extra={
6:[('Chemical regulation of gene expression','3,4,6,10,11,12,13,14,15,18,19,26,27,28,29','DNA methylation can silence a promoter without changing its base sequence. DNA tightly bound to histones is heterochromatic; loosely bound DNA is euchromatic. Differential epigenetic expression helps cells acquire different identities. Imprinting silences genes according to parental origin: the chromosome-15 deletion examples differ when paternal or maternal active copies are lost. Extra active IGF2 contributes to Beckwith-Wiedemann overgrowth; reduced IGF2 contributes to Russell-Silver growth restriction. Tumors can show overall hypomethylation with promoter hypermethylation of suppressor genes. Silencing MLH1 permits damaged DNA to accumulate. Small RNAs can degrade messenger RNA and prevent translation. Demethylating agents and histone deacetylase inhibitors target reversible epigenetic states. This chapter is in the assigned readings; not every component has its own numbered guide prompt.')],
12:[('Growth signals, repair and the microenvironment','21,22,23,24,25,30','Proto-oncogenes normally support regulated growth. Altered oncogenes can sustain growth signaling; tumor-suppressor loss removes restraints. A receptor stuck on can drive continued signaling. Caretaker genes repair DNA, so their loss increases mutation accumulation. Angiogenesis supplies new vessels. These are complementary mechanisms, not interchangeable names.')],
13:[('Exposure and interacting risk mechanisms','5,6,9,14,17','Genetic and epigenetic changes interact with the surrounding stroma. DNA repair, immune activity and metabolic factors modify susceptibility. Obesity increases insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia; adipose tissue secretes adipokines. Physical activity can decrease inflammatory mediators and insulin-like growth factors and improve immune function, with some protective associations independent of weight loss. Positive smoking associations do not imply that every unlisted cancer has no association.')],
14:[('Pediatric genetic and developmental associations','4,6,9,10N,11','The deck emphasizes leukemia and CNS tumors in children and lymphomas in adolescents. Embryonal tumors begin in immature prenatal tissues; many pediatric tumors arise from mesoderm. Notes connect N-myc with neuroblastoma, Gorlin syndrome with medulloblastoma, tuberous sclerosis with glial tumors and Down syndrome with leukemia. These limited associations do not establish a complete syndrome table. Etiology includes congenital, genetic, immune and exposure factors.')],
49:[('Recognize compensation before hypotension','5,6,17,18,21,22,23N,25,26,33,35,39','Normal systolic pressure can coexist with pediatric shock. Hypoxia is a common cause of bradycardia, and slowing may signal deterioration. Rising lactate can indicate impaired systemic perfusion; the printed numerical units are quarantined. Infants have high glucose needs and low glycogen stores. Renal and adrenergic responses conserve volume and redistribute blood, but newborn renal concentration is limited. Cold shock shows vasoconstriction, prolonged refill and weak pulses; warm shock shows vasodilation, bounding pulses and rapid refill. Neurogenic shock may lack tachycardia because sympathetic tone is lost. Obstructive shock requires relief of the impediment to flow.')]
}
